<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8" />
  <title>Large Sample HTML Page</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
  <style>
    * {
      box-sizing: border-box;
      margin: 0;
      padding: 0;
    }

    body {
      font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, "Segoe UI", sans-serif;
      line-height: 1.6;
      color: #222;
      background: #f5f5f7;
    }

    header {
      background: linear-gradient(135deg, #1f2933, #3b82f6);
      color: #fff;
      padding: 20px 40px;
      position: sticky;
      top: 0;
      z-index: 100;
    }

    header h1 {
      font-size: 28px;
      margin-bottom: 6px;
    }

    header p {
      font-size: 14px;
      opacity: 0.9;
    }

    nav {
      margin-top: 12px;
      display: flex;
      flex-wrap: wrap;
      gap: 10px;
    }

    nav a {
      color: #e5e7eb;
      text-decoration: none;
      font-size: 14px;
      padding: 6px 10px;
      border-radius: 999px;
      border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.25);
      transition: background 0.2s, color 0.2s;
    }

    nav a:hover {
      background: #fff;
      color: #1f2933;
    }

    main {
      max-width: 1200px;
      margin: 20px auto 60px;
      padding: 0 20px 40px;
    }

    section {
      background: #ffffff;
      border-radius: 12px;
      padding: 24px 24px 28px;
      margin-bottom: 24px;
      box-shadow: 0 10px 25px rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.08);
    }

    section h2 {
      font-size: 22px;
      margin-bottom: 10px;
      color: #111827;
    }

    section h3 {
      font-size: 18px;
      margin: 18px 0 8px;
      color: #111827;
    }

    section p {
      margin-bottom: 10px;
      font-size: 14px;
      color: #374151;
    }

    .grid-3 {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(260px, 1fr));
      gap: 16px;
      margin-top: 16px;
    }

    .card {
      border-radius: 10px;
      border: 1px solid #e5e7eb;
      padding: 16px;
      background: #f9fafb;
    }

    .card h4 {
      font-size: 16px;
      margin-bottom: 6px;
      color: #111827;
    }

    .card p {
      font-size: 13px;
      color: #4b5563;
    }

    .tag {
      display: inline-block;
      padding: 2px 8px;
      border-radius: 999px;
      font-size: 11px;
      background: #eff6ff;
      color: #1d4ed8;
      margin-right: 6px;
      margin-bottom: 6px;
    }

    table {
      width: 100%;
      border-collapse: collapse;
      margin-top: 12px;
      font-size: 13px;
    }

    table thead {
      background: #f3f4f6;
    }

    table th,
    table td {
      padding: 8px 10px;
      border: 1px solid #e5e7eb;
      text-align: left;
    }

    table th {
      font-weight: 600;
      color: #111827;
    }

    table tr:nth-child(even) {
      background: #f9fafb;
    }

    .code-block {
      background: #0b1120;
      color: #e5e7eb;
      padding: 14px 16px;
      border-radius: 8px;
      font-family: "SF Mono", Menlo, Monaco, Consolas, "Liberation Mono", "Courier New", monospace;
      font-size: 12px;
      overflow-x: auto;
      margin-top: 10px;
    }

    .code-block code {
      white-space: pre;
    }

    .columns-2 {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr));
      gap: 20px;
      margin-top: 16px;
    }

    .list {
      margin-top: 8px;
      margin-left: 18px;
      font-size: 14px;
      color: #374151;
    }

    .list li {
      margin-bottom: 6px;
    }

    .hero {
      display: flex;
      flex-wrap: wrap;
      gap: 20px;
      align-items: center;
      margin-top: 10px;
    }

    .hero-text {
      flex: 1 1 280px;
    }

    .hero-visual {
      flex: 1 1 260px;
      min-height: 180px;
      border-radius: 12px;
      background: radial-gradient(circle at top left, #3b82f6, #111827);
      position: relative;
      overflow: hidden;
    }

    .hero-dot {
      position: absolute;
      width: 10px;
      height: 10px;
      border-radius: 999px;
      background: #fbbf24;
      opacity: 0.9;
    }

    .hero-dot.dot1 {
      top: 24px;
      left: 30px;
    }

    .hero-dot.dot2 {
      bottom: 30px;
      right: 40px;
    }

    .hero-dot.dot3 {
      top: 50%;
      left: 60%;
    }

    .hero-visual-inner {
      position: absolute;
      inset: 18px;
      border-radius: 10px;
      border: 1px solid rgba(249, 250, 251, 0.3);
      background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.4);
      padding: 12px;
      color: #e5e7eb;
      font-size: 12px;
    }

    .hero-visual-inner h4 {
      font-size: 13px;
      margin-bottom: 6px;
    }

    .hero-visual-inner ul {
      margin-left: 16px;
      margin-top: 4px;
    }

    .hero-visual-inner li {
      margin-bottom: 4px;
    }

    .gallery {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(160px, 1fr));
      gap: 12px;
      margin-top: 14px;
    }

    .gallery-item {
      border-radius: 10px;
      background: #111827;
      color: #e5e7eb;
      padding: 10px;
      font-size: 12px;
      position: relative;
      overflow: hidden;
    }

    .gallery-item span {
      display: block;
      font-weight: 600;
      margin-bottom: 4px;
    }

    .gallery-item small {
      opacity: 0.8;
    }

    .gallery-item::after {
      content: "";
      position: absolute;
      inset: 0;
      background: radial-gradient(circle at top right, rgba(59, 130, 246, 0.4), transparent);
      opacity: 0.7;
      pointer-events: none;
    }

    .form-group {
      margin-bottom: 12px;
    }

    .form-group label {
      display: block;
      font-size: 13px;
      margin-bottom: 4px;
      color: #111827;
    }

    .form-group input,
    .form-group select,
    .form-group textarea {
      width: 100%;
      padding: 8px 10px;
      border-radius: 6px;
      border: 1px solid #d1d5db;
      font-size: 13px;
      font-family: inherit;
    }

    .form-group textarea {
      min-height: 80px;
      resize: vertical;
    }

    .btn-row {
      display: flex;
      gap: 10px;
      margin-top: 10px;
    }

    .btn {
      padding: 8px 14px;
      border-radius: 999px;
      border: none;
      cursor: pointer;
      font-size: 13px;
      font-weight: 500;
      display: inline-flex;
      align-items: center;
      gap: 6px;
    }

    .btn-primary {
      background: #2563eb;
      color: #f9fafb;
    }

    .btn-secondary {
      background: #e5e7eb;
      color: #111827;
    }

    .btn-outline {
      background: transparent;
      border: 1px solid #9ca3af;
      color: #374151;
    }

    .badge {
      display: inline-block;
      padding: 2px 8px;
      border-radius: 999px;
      font-size: 11px;
      background: #ecfeff;
      color: #0f766e;
      margin-left: 6px;
    }

    footer {
      text-align: center;
      padding: 20px;
      font-size: 12px;
      color: #6b7280;
    }

    @media (max-width: 640px) {
      header {
        padding: 16px 18px;
      }

      main {
        padding: 0 14px 30px;
      }

      section {
        padding: 18px 16px 22px;
      }
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <header>
    <h1>Large HTML Demo Page</h1>
    <p>A single file with many sections, components, and layouts you can tweak or extend.</p>
    <nav>
      <a href="#intro">Intro</a>
      <a href="#features">Features</a>
      <a href="#cards">Cards</a>
      <a href="#table">Table</a>
      <a href="#code">Code</a>
      <a href="#layout">Layout</a>
      <a href="#gallery">Gallery</a>
      <a href="#forms">Forms</a>
      <a href="#long-text">Long Text</a>
    </nav>
  </header>

  <main>
    <!-- Intro Section -->
    <section id="intro">
      <h2>Intro section</h2>
      <div class="hero">
        <div class="hero-text">
          <p>
            This HTML file is intentionally large and structured. You can use it as a playground,
            a template, or a starting point for a more complex project. It includes navigation,
            cards, tables, forms, and multiple layout patterns.
          </p>
          <p>
            Feel free to remove sections you don’t need, rename components, or wire them up to
            JavaScript later. The goal is to give you a rich, but still readable, base.
          </p>
          <div>
            <span class="tag">HTML5</span>
            <span class="tag">CSS-only</span>
            <span class="tag">Responsive</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="hero-visual">
          <div class="hero-dot dot1"></div>
          <div class="hero-dot dot2"></div>
          <div class="hero-dot dot3"></div>
          <div class="hero-visual-inner">
            <h4>Page structure overview</h4>
            <ul>
              <li>Sticky header with navigation</li>
              <li>Multiple content sections</li>
              <li>Cards, tables, forms, and more</li>
              <li>Responsive grid layouts</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Features Section -->
    <section id="features">
      <h2>Feature overview</h2>
      <p>
        Below is a high-level overview of what this page demonstrates. Each feature is implemented
        with simple, modern HTML and CSS—no frameworks, no external dependencies.
      </p>

      <div class="grid-3">
        <div class="card">
          <h4>Semantic sections</h4>
          <p>
            The page uses semantic tags like <code>&lt;header&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;main&gt;</code>,
            and <code>&lt;section&gt;</code> to keep the structure clear and accessible.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h4>Responsive grids</h4>
          <p>
            CSS grid is used for cards, galleries, and multi-column layouts. It adapts to different
            screen sizes without extra media queries.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h4>Reusable components</h4>
          <p>
            Cards, buttons, tags, and form groups are styled in a reusable way so you can copy them
            into other projects easily.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="grid-3" style="margin-top: 18px;">
        <div class="card">
          <h4>Typography</h4>
          <p>
            The typography is kept simple and legible, using system fonts and modest sizes to avoid
            visual noise.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h4>Tables</h4>
          <p>
            A styled table section shows how to present structured data with alternating row colors
            and clear headers.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h4>Forms</h4>
          <p>
            The form section includes text inputs, selects, textareas, and button groups, ready to
            be connected to your backend or JavaScript.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Cards Section -->
    <section id="cards">
      <h2>Cards and tags</h2>
      <p>
        This section demonstrates a larger set of cards. You can imagine these representing
        projects, articles, or modules in an application.
      </p>

      <div class="grid-3">
        <div class="card">
          <h4>Project Alpha <span class="badge">Active</span></h4>
          <p>
            A foundational project focused on building core components and establishing design
            patterns that other modules can reuse.
          </p>
          <ul class="list">
            <li>Core UI library</li>
            <li>Accessibility baseline</li>
            <li>Documentation starter</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h4>Project Beta <span class="badge">Planning</span></h4>
          <p>
            A research-heavy initiative exploring new interaction models, performance optimizations,
            and user feedback loops.
          </p>
          <ul class="list">
            <li>User interviews</li>
            <li>Prototype testing</li>
            <li>Performance benchmarks</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h4>Project Gamma <span class="badge">Backlog</span></h4>
          <p>
            A long-term idea bank for experimental features that may or may not ship, but keep
            creativity alive.
          </p>
          <ul class="list">
            <li>Experimental layouts</li>
            <li>Micro-animations</li>
            <li>Advanced theming</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Table Section -->
    <section id="table">
      <h2>Data table example</h2>
      <p>
        Here is a simple table representing a fictional roadmap. You can replace the content with
        real data, or expand the columns and rows as needed.
      </p>

      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Item</th>
            <th>Status</th>
            <th>Owner</th>
            <th>ETA</th>
            <th>Notes</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Navigation redesign</td>
            <td>In progress</td>
            <td>Design team</td>
            <td>Q1</td>
            <td>Focus on clarity and mobile usability.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Performance audit</td>
            <td>Planned</td>
            <td>Engineering</td>
            <td>Q2</td>
            <td>Measure and improve load times and responsiveness.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Accessibility review</td>
            <td>Completed</td>
            <td>UX</td>
            <td>Q4 (last year)</td>
            <td>Baseline WCAG compliance achieved.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Content refresh</td>
            <td>In progress</td>
            <td>Content team</td>
            <td>Q3</td>
            <td>Update copy and visuals to match new brand.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Internationalization</td>
            <td>Backlog</td>
            <td>Localization</td>
            <td>TBD</td>
            <td>Requires language strategy and translation pipeline.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </section>

    <!-- Code Section -->
    <section id="code">
      <h2>Code block example</h2>
      <p>
        This section shows how you might present code snippets inside a styled block. You can
        replace this with your own HTML, CSS, or JavaScript examples.
      </p>

      <div class="code-block">
        <code>
&lt;!-- Basic card component --&gt;
&lt;div class="card"&gt;
  &lt;h4&gt;Card title&lt;/h4&gt;
  &lt;p&gt;
    This is a reusable card component. You can drop it into any grid or layout.
  &lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;!-- Simple button group --&gt;
&lt;div class="btn-row"&gt;
  &lt;button class="btn btn-primary"&gt;Save&lt;/button&gt;
  &lt;button class="btn btn-secondary"&gt;Cancel&lt;/button&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
        </code>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Layout Section -->
    <section id="layout">
      <h2>Two-column layout</h2>
      <p>
        This section uses a two-column grid to separate conceptual content from implementation
        details. It’s a common pattern for documentation or dashboards.
      </p>

      <div class="columns-2">
        <div>
          <h3>Conceptual overview</h3>
          <p>
            When designing a page with many sections, it helps to think in terms of reusable
            patterns. Cards, tables, and forms are all patterns that can be combined in different
            ways depending on the context.
          </p>
          <p>
            A large HTML file like this can serve as a living style guide. As you refine your
            components, you can keep them here and copy them into new pages when needed.
          </p>
          <ul class="list">
            <li>Identify common patterns</li>
            <li>Keep styles consistent</li>
            <li>Document usage with examples</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Implementation notes</h3>
          <p>
            The CSS in this file is intentionally compact but expressive. You can split it into
            separate files, convert it to a preprocessor, or integrate it into a component system.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you add JavaScript, consider keeping behavior modular—attach scripts to specific
            sections or components rather than the entire page at once.
          </p>
          <ul class="list">
            <li>Use classes for reusable styles</li>
            <li>Keep selectors simple and predictable</li>
            <li>Refactor into modules as the project grows</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Gallery Section -->
    <section id="gallery">
      <h2>Text-based gallery</h2>
      <p>
        Instead of images, this gallery uses text blocks to represent different “tiles.” You can
        swap these for real images or icons later.
      </p>

      <div class="gallery">
        <div class="gallery-item">
          <span>Tile A</span>
          <small>Represents a feature, article, or dataset.</small>
        </div>
        <div class="gallery-item">
          <span>Tile B</span>
          <small>Could be a quick link to a report or dashboard.</small>
        </div>
        <div class="gallery-item">
          <span>Tile C</span>
          <small>Maybe a shortcut to a frequently used tool.</small>
        </div>
        <div class="gallery-item">
          <span>Tile D</span>
          <small>Placeholder for anything you want to highlight.</small>
        </div>
        <div class="gallery-item">
          <span>Tile E</span>
          <small>Another slot for content or navigation.</small>
        </div>
        <div class="gallery-item">
          <span>Tile F</span>
          <small>Use this to experiment with layout variations.</small>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Forms Section -->
    <section id="forms">
      <h2>Form section</h2>
      <p>
        This form is not wired to any backend—it’s just HTML and CSS. You can hook it up to your
        own endpoints or JavaScript handlers.
      </p>

      <form>
        <div class="form-group">
          <label for="name">Name</label>
          <input id="name" type="text" placeholder="Enter your name" />
        </div>

        <div class="form-group">
          <label for="email">Email</label>
          <input id="email" type="email" placeholder="you@example.com" />
        </div>

        <div class="form-group">
          <label for="role">Role</label>
          <select id="role">
            <option value="">Select a role</option>
            <option value="designer">Designer</option>
            <option value="developer">Developer</option>
            <option value="pm">Product manager</option>
            <option value="other">Other</option>
          </select>
        </div>

        <div class="form-group">
          <label for="message">Message</label>
          <textarea id="message" placeholder="Write a short message..."></textarea>
        </div>

        <div class="btn-row">
          <button type="submit" class="btn btn-primary">Submit</button>
          <button type="reset" class="btn btn-secondary">Reset</button>
          <button type="button" class="btn btn-outline">Preview</button>
        </div>
      </form>
    </section>

    <!-- Long Text Section -->
    <section id="long-text">
      <h2>Long text content</h2>
      <p>
        Sometimes you just need a lot of text to see how a layout behaves. This section is here
        for that purpose. You can replace it with real documentation, an article, or any content
        that needs more vertical space.
      </p>
      <p>
        As you scroll through a long section, pay attention to how the header behaves, how the
        spacing feels, and whether the typography remains comfortable to read. Small adjustments
        to line height, font size, and margins can make a big difference in perceived quality.
      </p>
      <p>
        You might also experiment with subheadings, blockquotes, or callout boxes. Those elements
        can help break up dense content and guide the reader’s attention to what matters most.
      </p>
      <p>
        In a real project, this section could be a changelog, a knowledge base article, or a
        narrative walkthrough of a feature. The key is that the layout should support both short
        and long content gracefully.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you decide to turn this page into a template, consider which sections are optional and
        which are core. Optional sections can be toggled on or off depending on the page’s purpose,
        while core sections remain consistent across the site.
      </p>
      <p>
        Finally, remember that a large HTML file is just a starting point. Over time, you’ll
        likely split it into smaller components, partials, or templates. That’s a good thing—it
        means the design is evolving and becoming more maintainable.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>

  <footer>
    Large HTML demo page &mdash; customize, trim, or extend it however you like.
  </footer>
</body>
</html>
